Annotations: |
  This cell owns the two manager objects that drive the extension-point chains defined in
  plugins/pairlist and plugins/protections: building the configured chain of handlers (via
  `PairListResolver` and `ProtectionResolver`) and running it against live state every bot
  iteration.

---

"PairListManager(exchange: Exchange, config: dict[str, Any], dataprovider: DataProvider | None)":
  location: pairlistmanager.py
  annotations: |
    Owns the configured chain of `IPairList` handlers and the resulting whitelist/blacklist.

    Algorithm:
    1. Build each configured pairlist handler via PairListResolver.load_pairlist
    2. On refresh_pairlist, run the position-0 handler's gen_pairlist, then each subsequent
       handler's filter_pairlist, in configured order
  properties:
    "whitelist -> list[str]": |
      Current tradeable pair whitelist, as of the last refresh_pairlist call.
    "blacklist -> list[str]": |
      Configured pair blacklist (supports wildcards, expanded via plugins/pairlist's
      expand_pairlist).
    "expanded_blacklist -> list[str]": |
      blacklist with wildcards expanded against currently known markets.
    "name_list -> list[str]": |
      Class names of the configured handler chain, in order.
  methods:
    "refresh_pairlist(only_first: bool, pairs: list[str] | None)": |
      Re-run the handler chain to recompute whitelist.

      `only_first`: run only the generator (position-0) handler, skipping filters
      `pairs`: run the chain starting from this explicit list instead of generating one
    "verify_blacklist(pairlist: list[str], logmethod: Any) -> pairlist:list[str]": |
      Remove blacklisted pairs from `pairlist`.
    "verify_whitelist(pairlist: list[str], logmethod: Any, keep_invalid: bool) -> pairlist:list[str]": |
      Remove pairs from `pairlist` not currently tradeable on exchange.

"ProtectionManager(config: dict[str, Any], protections: list[dict[str, Any]])":
  location: protectionmanager.py
  annotations: |
    Owns the configured chain of `IProtection` handlers and runs them after every trade close.

    Algorithm:
    1. Build each configured protection handler via ProtectionResolver.load_protection
    2. On global_stop/stop_per_pair, run every handler that declares the matching
       has_global_stop/has_local_stop capability and lock via the first one that returns a
       ProtectionReturn
  properties:
    "name_list -> list[str]": |
      Class names of the configured handler chain, in order.
  methods:
    "global_stop(now: datetime | None, side: str, starting_balance: float) -> lock:PairLock | None": |
      Run every global-capable handler; persist and return a `PairLock` if any requests one.
    "stop_per_pair(pair: str, now: datetime | None, side: str, starting_balance: float) -> lock:PairLock | None": |
      Run every per-pair-capable handler for `pair`; persist and return a `PairLock` if any
      requests one.
    "short_desc() -> descriptions:list[dict[str, Any]]": |
      One-line description per configured handler, for /pairlists-style API responses.
    "validate_protections(protections: list[dict[str, Any]])": |
      Validate a raw `protections` config list before building handlers from it (e.g. reject
      unknown handler names or malformed parameters).

